rue,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,true,299,true,false,true,7]}],"solution":"class RandomizedSet:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.vals = []      # the elements, in arbitrary order\n        self.pos = {}       # value -> index in self.vals\n\n    def insert(self, val: int) -> bool:\n        if val in self.pos:\n            return False\n        self.pos[val] = len(self.vals)\n        self.vals.append(val)\n        return True\n\n    def remove(self, val: int) -> bool:\n        if val not in self.pos:\n            return False\n        i, last = self.pos[val], self.vals[-1]\n        self.vals[i] = last\n        self.pos[last] = i\n        self.vals.pop()\n        del self.pos[val]\n        return True\n\n    def getRandom(self) -> int:\n        return random.choice(self.vals)\n","approach":"**Key insight: a dynamic array plus an index map.** Uniform random choice needs O(1) access by position, which only an array provides. Deleting from the middle of an array is O(n), unless you do not care about order: overwrite the removed slot with the last element and pop the tail.\n\n`vals` holds the elements; `pos[v]` is the index of `v` inside `vals`. Insert appends and records the index. Remove looks up the index `i`, copies the last element into slot `i`, updates that element's index to `i`, pops the list and deletes the removed value from the map. `getRandom` is `random.choice(vals)`, which is uniform because every element occupies exactly one slot.\n\n**Common mistakes:** updating the map for the moved element *after* deleting the removed key when both are the same value (removing the last element); when `val` is the tail, the swap is a no-op and the code must still work, which the order above guarantees. Using `list.remove(val)` or `list.index(val)` silently makes remove O(n).\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Allow duplicates (`RandomizedCollection`): map each value to a *set* of indices. Weighted random pick: prefix sums plus binary search, O(log n) per pick. Thread safety: a single lock is fine; lock-free variants are hard because remove mutates two structures that must stay consistent.","complexity":{"time":"O(1) average per operation","space":"O(n)"},"similar":["lru-cache","design-hit-counter"],"number":178}